body:
Consider this [WPT](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/html/dom/render-blocking/document-render-blocking.tentative.html) where the author adds the following style:

```css
#last {
   view-transition-name: foo;
}
```

If the browser yields after the first `jankMany` script block (after parsing line 26), it will do a render before the node with id "last" is parsed. When the style cascade is done, no node with id "last" will be found so we don't see "foo" in the list of `view-transition-name`s on this Document on first render.

So how can the browser know which `view-transition-name`s are supposed to be found in the new document without parsing until the end? Or without an API for the author to provide this list at a point when rendering is guaranteed to be blocked (which is until `body` is added to the DOM today).

> While it's possible that the developer made a mistake and we'd hold parsing till the end of the document (or a reasonable timeout), that seems better than telling developers to block incremental parsing by default. (and jump through hoops to unblock it)

I want to rephrase that to make sure we're on the same page. This proposal is not about disabling incremental rendering completely. It's about developers having some input into when the page is rendered, as opposed to doing it using browser heuristics only.

Correct me if I misunderstood, your idea does acknowledge that the browser needs to wait for the requisite elements (with a `view-transition-name` for this transition) to be parsed. The question is whether that's done by the developer providing us an explicit list, or the browser does it automagically. The assumption being that doing it automagically is less error prone. But I don't think that assumption holds true because its quite difficult for the browser to deduce which style rules will finally apply.

Consider the simple case where the author uses a class name to apply some names via an A/B experiment.

```html
<style>
   .group-a #last {
      view-transition-name: foo;
   }
</style>
<script>
   if (computeUserGroup() == "a") {
      document.documentElement.classList.add("group-a");
   }
</script>
```

The style system sees the selector but can't know if the class name will appear in the DOM, author may be using script to conditionally add it. So we'll inadvertently block rendering until the full html is parsed. You could argue that the author could omit styles or conditionally fetch stylesheets after assigning the user a group. But now we're forcing authors to be very cautious about which stylesheets they use so the browser computes the right list of `view-transition-name`s to wait for. That seems more error prone to me than an explicit list.

@lilles @tabatkins will probably have a more definitive style opinion. But I don't think its feasible to figure out which names will eventually be applied to nodes in the style cascade just by looking at the style rules.

> The A/B testing case is an interesting one, as developers will not be able to provide a markup based list that may or may not include elements for us to wait for. (unless that's done with script, which adds complexity)

If you're using an A/B testing framework for UX of your page, you're likely dealing with more complex script to setup the DOM than what's needed to setup a list of blocking elements. I'd expect it could be as simple as choosing between 2 static lists.

```js
function listOfBlockingElements() {
   if (userInGroupA()) {
      return [id1, id2];
   } else {
      return [id1];
   }
}
```

>> We previously talked about waiting only for the names that the old page had to make sure paired transition worked and entry animations that were not in the first render are "ok to skip".

> I agree, and I think this is where we want the developer's input - distinguishing those 2 types.

@yoavweiss, Vlad's comment still implies the author is giving us an explicit list of names to look for. In this suggestion, the list is coming from the old Document. The browser knows which names were captured, and we'll be adding a new hook for the author to tag which names are exit vs paired animations. What happens if the developer says something is paired but we don't find a counterpart in the new Document? We could fail the transition but that seems too harsh since by design that can happen because of the browser timeout. And authors can design a reasonable fallback for such cases (like just using the new root for the animation).

This is interesting because I could see an author intentionally marking an exit animation as paired to block full parsing, if they are fixing a bug with an entry animation not working. Vs an API that lets the author give us a list of entry `view-transition-name`s as well so they don't have to try to hack around it.

----
Also before diving further into alternate solutions, can we resolve on the fact that the browser computing the list of names to block on by inspecting style rules is _not feasible_? This problem requires an API for authors to specify which elements should block first render. That list could come from old or new Document, the element could be identified using html attributes or computed style state and we might choose to solve it for only a subset of animation types.
